# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.
# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.
# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.
GEMINI_API_KEY="MY_GEMINI_API_KEY"

# GROQ_API_KEY: Optional free-tier fallback for the AI booking bot (WhatsApp + email),
# used only when both Gemini models fail (daily quota exhausted, or a transient 503
# overload). Sign up free at https://console.groq.com -> API Keys -> Create API Key.
# No card required. Without this set, the bot just surfaces the Gemini error as before.
GROQ_API_KEY=""

# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.
# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.
# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.
APP_URL="MY_APP_URL"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-tenant platform (optional, but required together for a real deployment
# used by multiple organizations — each org signs up, logs in, and configures
# its own WhatsApp/Email/Calendar credentials via the in-app Settings dashboard
# instead of editing this file). Without these, the app still runs standalone
# for a single business, same as before.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 1) Supabase — stores accounts (Supabase Auth), org settings, leads' bot
#    conversations, and org->calendar links. Create a free project at
#    https://supabase.com, run supabase/schema.sql in its SQL Editor, then
#    paste these from Project Settings -> API:
#      - SUPABASE_URL / SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY: server-only, full access, NEVER expose to the browser.
#      - VITE_SUPABASE_URL / VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY: safe for the browser (RLS restricts each org to its own rows).
#    Team invites are sent by Supabase Auth's own "Invite user" email. In Supabase ->
#    Authentication -> URL Configuration, set Site URL to APP_URL and add APP_URL to the
#    Redirect URLs, so the invite link opens this app (where the invitee sets a password).
SUPABASE_URL=""
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=""
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

# Random secret used to sign the Google OAuth "state" param so the callback can
# trust which org initiated the connect request. Any long random string.
OAUTH_STATE_SECRET=""

# 2) Meta WhatsApp webhook verification — pick any random string yourself, put
#    it here AND paste the same string into every org's Meta App Dashboard ->
#    WhatsApp -> Configuration -> Webhook -> Verify Token (one shared webhook
#    URL handles all orgs; each org points their own Meta app at it):
#    https://<your-domain>/api/whatsapp/webhook (subscribe to the "messages" field).
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=""

# Note: signature verification (X-Hub-Signature-256) is per-org, not a server env var here —
# each org has their own separate Meta App, so a single shared secret can't verify all of
# them. Each org pastes their own App Secret in Settings -> WhatsApp Cloud API instead
# (kept server-side in org_channel_secrets). It's required: incoming messages for an org
# with no App Secret saved are rejected. See lib/whatsappWebhookHandler.ts.

# 3) Google Calendar — one platform-level OAuth Client ID/Secret lets every org
#    click "Connect Google Calendar" in the dashboard and authorize their own
#    calendar (each org's own refresh token is stored separately, per-org, in
#    Supabase — nothing calendar-specific goes in this file per org).
#      a) Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Enable "Google Calendar API"
#      b) Create OAuth 2.0 Client ID, type "Web application", add authorized
#         redirect URI: <APP_URL>/api/auth/google/callback
#         (for local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback)
#      c) "Connect Gmail" (sending email from an org's own mailbox) uses this same client:
#         also enable the "Gmail API", and add the gmail.send scope on the OAuth consent
#         screen. gmail.send is a sensitive scope — until Google verifies the app, only test
#         users you list on the consent screen can connect (others see "unverified app").
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI="http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback"

# 3b) Outlook / Microsoft 365 Calendar — same one-platform-app, many-orgs-connect-their-own
#    pattern as Google Calendar above. An org connects at most one of the two; whichever is
#    connected is what the AI booking bot uses (see lib/calendarProvider.ts).
#      a) Azure Portal -> Microsoft Entra ID -> App registrations -> New registration.
#      b) Supported account types: MUST be "Accounts in any organizational directory (Any
#         Microsoft Entra ID tenant) and personal Microsoft accounts" — NOT "My organization
#         only", or every client outside your own tenant will be rejected at sign-in.
#      c) Authentication -> Add a platform -> Web -> redirect URI:
#         <APP_URL>/api/auth/microsoft/callback (local dev:
#         http://localhost:3000/api/auth/microsoft/callback). This path is served by
#         api/auth/google/callback.ts via a vercel.json rewrite (?provider=microsoft) to stay
#         under Vercel Hobby's 12-serverless-function cap — not a bug if you go looking for a
#         separate microsoft/callback.ts file and don't find one.
#      d) Certificates & secrets -> New client secret -> copy the Value immediately (shown once).
#      e) API permissions -> Add a permission -> Microsoft Graph -> Delegated: Calendars.ReadWrite,
#         Mail.Send (for "Connect Outlook" email sending), offline_access, User.Read.
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=""
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=""
MICROSOFT_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI="http://localhost:3000/api/auth/microsoft/callback"

# 4a) Outbound email sending — platform-level Resend account, shared by every org (an org
#     only needs its own key in Settings if it wants to send from its own domain instead).
#       a) Sign up free at https://resend.com (no card required for the free tier).
#       b) Dashboard -> API Keys -> Create API Key with *Full access* (orgs add their own
#          sending domains through it — a "Sending access" key can't manage domains) -> paste below.
#       c) Optional: Dashboard -> Domains -> Add Domain -> verify quardlink.com (a few
#          TXT/CNAME records at your DNS host — doesn't touch MX/inbox records). Until
#          verified, RESEND_FROM_ADDRESS falls back to onboarding@resend.dev, which can
#          only deliver to the Resend account's own signup email (fine for first tests).
#       d) Each org can add its own domain in Settings -> Email (DNS records shown in-app). Mail
#          from an org goes out from its own domain once verified, else from RESEND_FROM_ADDRESS
#          — never from another org's domain.
RESEND_API_KEY=""
RESEND_FROM_ADDRESS="onboarding@resend.dev"
# Bounces and spam complaints (lib/emailBounces.ts): in Resend → Webhooks, add
#   https://<your-domain>/api/email/inbound?action=resend-events
# with the email.bounced and email.complained events, and paste its signing secret here.
# Bounced addresses go on the org's do-not-contact list; a complaint opts the lead out.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# 4b) Inbound email replies (AI email booking bot) — platform-level setup, done once by
#    whoever runs this app, using a domain you control. Two options:
#      - Cloudflare Email Routing (free, no volume limits) — see cloudflare/email-worker/README.md
#        for the full setup. EMAIL_INBOUND_DOMAIN is just your root domain (e.g. yourdomain.com).
#      - SendGrid Inbound Parse (requires a paid SendGrid plan):
#          a) Pick a subdomain for receiving mail, e.g. inbound.yourdomain.com
#          b) Add an MX record for that subdomain pointing to mx.sendgrid.net (priority 10)
#          c) In SendGrid: Settings -> Inbound Parse -> Add Host & URL
#               Receiving domain: inbound.yourdomain.com
#               Destination URL:  https://<your-app-domain>/api/email/inbound?token=<EMAIL_INBOUND_WEBHOOK_SECRET below>
#    Either way, campaign emails automatically get a Reply-To like reply+cr_<id>@<EMAIL_INBOUND_DOMAIN>
#    so a client's reply routes back to this app, and the AI replies using each org's own
#    configured "From" identity (Resend/SendGrid/SMTP/Mailgun).
#    Without this, outbound campaign email still works — only automated reply-handling needs it.
EMAIL_INBOUND_DOMAIN="yourdomain.com"
EMAIL_INBOUND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# 5) Country peak-time campaign scheduling — authenticates /api/cron/dispatch-scheduled,
#    which sends any campaign messages that were scheduled (per-client, by country/local
#    business hours) instead of sent immediately. Any long random string, but the name
#    must be exactly CRON_SECRET — Vercel automatically attaches it as a Bearer header to
#    its own cron invocations (see the "crons" entry in vercel.json) when named this way.
#    A free external pinger (e.g. cron-job.org) can also call the same URL with
#    ?token=<this value> for finer-grained scheduling than Vercel Hobby's 1x/day cron cap.
#    This same run also places AI-call retries for leads who didn't answer and sends the
#    WhatsApp/email follow-up after the last try (lib/voiceFollowUp.ts), so retries are only
#    as punctual as it runs:
#      - Vercel Hobby (testing): the built-in cron runs once a day — point cron-job.org at
#        https://<your-domain>/api/cron/dispatch-scheduled?token=<CRON_SECRET> every 10 minutes.
#      - Vercel Pro: change the "schedule" in vercel.json's crons entry to "* * * * *" (every
#        minute) and drop the external pinger. (Hobby rejects any schedule more often than daily.)
#    It also sends server-run WhatsApp/email campaigns (lib/messageQueue.ts) — each run sends
#    for ~35 seconds at the campaign's pace, so running it every minute keeps campaigns moving
#    when nobody has the Campaigns page open (with the page open, the page itself keeps them going).
#    Testing call follow-ups only? Add &only=voice to the URL — that run places due call
#    retries and nothing else (no campaign sends, no retries of failed campaign messages).
CRON_SECRET=""

# 6) Vapi (vapi.ai) AI voice calling — one platform Vapi account, one bill. Each org gets a
#    dedicated assistant automatically the first time it saves a voice prompt, and its OWN
#    phone number — there is no shared number. Sign up at https://vapi.ai, then:
#      a) VAPI_API_KEY: Dashboard -> API Keys -> use the *Private* key (never the Public one).
#      b) VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID: a template assistant — new org assistants copy its model/voice.
#      c) VAPI_WEBHOOK_SECRET: any long random string. Every call (and every number you
#         assign) is pointed at <APP_URL>/api/voice/vapi?action=webhook with this secret
#         automatically — no Server URL to set by hand in Vapi's dashboard.
#      d) Phone numbers: buy/import one per org in Vapi's dashboard (Phone Numbers), then
#         assign it to the org from Sidebar -> Admin. Assigning also makes the AI answer
#         calls to that number (APP_URL must be the public https URL for that). Orgs on their
#         own Vapi account turn incoming calls on themselves in Settings -> Voice.
#      e) VAPI_MAX_CONCURRENT_CALLS: how many calls your Vapi plan allows at the same time,
#         shared by every client on the platform (Vapi's default is 10; raise it here when you
#         buy more). Voice campaigns queue behind this (lib/callQueue.ts) instead of failing.
VAPI_API_KEY=""
VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID=""
VAPI_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
VAPI_MAX_CONCURRENT_CALLS="10"

# 7) Vapi Web SDK — powers the Dashboard's "Live Voice Demo" widget, a real voice
#    conversation held straight in the browser (WebRTC to the assistant) rather than an
#    outbound phone call, so it works regardless of Vapi plan tier or the lead's country.
#      a) VITE_VAPI_PUBLIC_KEY: Vapi dashboard -> API Keys -> the *Public* key (never the
#         Private one — the public key is designed to be safe in browser code; it can only
#         start calls with assistants you own, nothing account-level).
#      b) VITE_VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID: same assistant as VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID above (assistant ids
#         aren't sensitive, safe to duplicate here for the browser to read).
#    VITE_-prefixed vars are bundled into the client build — that's intentional for both of
#    these. Without them, the widget just shows a "not configured" hint instead of a button.
VITE_VAPI_PUBLIC_KEY=""
VITE_VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID=""

# 8) Admin dashboard — no self-serve subscription checkout by design. A prospect contacts
#    you directly, you agree a tier, payment happens outside the app, and you manually flip
#    their workspace active from Sidebar -> Admin. Every new signup starts 'locked' (see
#    on_auth_user_created_access in supabase/schema.sql) except this email, which always
#    stays active. Set the SAME value in both — ADMIN_EMAIL is what's actually enforced
#    server-side; VITE_ADMIN_EMAIL only controls whether the Admin nav item shows up.
ADMIN_EMAIL=""
VITE_ADMIN_EMAIL=""

# 9) CRM integrations (lib/crm/*) — HubSpot, Zoho CRM and Salesforce. Same pattern as the
#    calendar connections: you register ONE app per CRM for the platform, and each client
#    clicks "Connect" in Channel Setup → CRM to authorise their own account. Set up only the
#    CRMs you want to offer; the others don't appear. All three use the same redirect URL:
#      <APP_URL>/api/auth/crm/callback   (local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/crm/callback)
#    APP_URL must be set for this (or set CRM_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI explicitly).
#
#    HubSpot — developers.hubspot.com → Apps → Create app → Auth: add the redirect URL and the
#      scopes crm.objects.contacts.read, crm.objects.contacts.write (plus oauth). Copy the
#      Client ID / Client secret. Clients outside your account need the app to be public or
#      installed via its install URL — HubSpot's "unverified app" notice applies until listed.
HUBSPOT_CLIENT_ID=""
HUBSPOT_CLIENT_SECRET=""
#    Zoho CRM — api-console.zoho.com → Add Client → "Server-based Applications": add the
#      redirect URL, then Settings → enable "Multi-DC" so clients on zoho.in / .eu / .com.au /
#      .sa etc. can connect (each one's data centre is detected automatically).
ZOHO_CLIENT_ID=""
ZOHO_CLIENT_SECRET=""
#    Salesforce — Setup → App Manager → New Connected App (or External Client App): enable
#      OAuth, add the redirect URL, scopes "api" and "refresh_token, offline_access", and allow
#      the refresh token to be valid until revoked. Optional SALESFORCE_LOGIN_URL for sandboxes
#      (https://test.salesforce.com).
SALESFORCE_CLIENT_ID=""
SALESFORCE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
SALESFORCE_LOGIN_URL=""
